<section class="section" id="idea">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2 class="h-display max-w-[20ch]" data-reveal>
      Every one-off scraper gets written twice.
    </h2>

    <hr class="rule my-10" data-rule />

    <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-10">
      <p class="copy" data-reveal>
        Once to find the selectors by hand, and again when the site changes. The
        second write is the expensive one: it happens under time pressure, in
        code someone else wrote, after the data has already gone quietly wrong.
      </p>
      <p class="copy" data-reveal>
        reapfield pays a language model <strong>once per field per domain</strong
        > to discover the CSS selectors, caches them, and replays them
        deterministically forever after. It goes back to the model only when a
        cached selector actually stops working.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p
      class="mt-10 text-sub font-semibold leading-[1.25] tracking-[-0.02em]"
      data-reveal
    >
      Steady-state cost is <span class="text-free">zero LLM calls</span>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
